The jump cache survives a TLB flush, and holds 65,536 entries.

Every TLB flush cleared the jump cache (tcg_flush_jmp_cache in
tlb_flush_by_mmuidx_async_work), because a cached entry maps a virtual
pc to a TB and nothing re-checked that mapping.  Windows 98 reloads CR3
with its own value 2,400 times a second while 3DMark 99 runs (its VMM's
TLB flush after every page it maps or unmaps), so every `ret`, `call *`
and `jmp *` after each of them missed the inline probe and paid the
hash-table lookup: qht_lookup_custom, tb_htable_lookup and
tb_lookup_cmp were 2.3 % of the first-person test and the main-loop
round trips around them another 2 %.

An entry's key is now the pc with the cache's generation in the high
half of the word (a 32-bit target's pc leaves it free; a 64-bit one
keeps the clear), a flush bumps the generation, and tb_lookup()
re-validates an entry of an older generation by the check the hash
table makes -- the TB's physical page(s) against what the pc maps to
now -- and re-stamps it.  In the first-person window not one of 2.3
million revalidations a second failed.  The cache is also 65,536
entries instead of 4,096: with flushes free it could grow, and the
misses that remained were conflicts, 11.7 million to 2.5 million per
ten seconds.

Measured on 3DMark 99 (claude98, Win98 SE, our display driver, TCG on
the Ryzen 5700X): first person 18.2 -> 19.0 fps.  Switch:
`-accel tcg,jump-cache-keep=off` restores the clear (the cache size is
compiled in).  SSE / x87 / SMC / rep batteries identical,
scripts/test.sh all green.

